WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.820
When you think of data centers, you might
be imagining rows of powerful

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.740
buzzing machines. But what's actually
running inside those

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.520
machines? And how do organizations

4
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.140
make the most of their hardware? That's
where

5
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.840
understanding physical versus virtual
infrastructure

6
00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.700
becomes essential. Let's start with
physical

7
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.200
or bare metal infrastructure. In this
setup,

8
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.420
each piece of hardware runs one operating
system,

9
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.880
which in turn runs one application. This
is known as

10
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.800
a one-to-one model. It's simple and
direct, but not

11
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.740
always efficient. In many traditional
deployments, you

12
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.640
might have powerful servers running at
only fifteen or thirty

13
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.620
percent of their capacity. That's a lot of

14
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.600
unused compute power, especially when
hardware, power, and

15
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.300
cooling costs add up. In fact, low

16
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.920
utilization can be so persistent that some
servers

17
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.380
become what the industry calls zombie
servers.

18
00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.340
These are machines that are still powered
on using electricity and

19
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.580
cooling, but no longer perform any
meaningful work.

20
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.680
Over time, they lose their original
function, but stay online,

21
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.220
draining resources without adding any
value.

22
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.740
To improve efficiency, organizations often
move to

23
00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.820
virtualization. This is where one single
physical

24
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.060
server can host multiple virtual machines,

25
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.280
each with its own operating system and
application. It's

26
00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.020
like turning one powerful machine into
many smaller ones.

27
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.480
Virtualization is made possible by a
software layer called a hypervisor.

28
00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.880
It divides the hardware resources like
processing, memory, and

29
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.800
storage amongst multiple virtual
environments.

30
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.580
Each virtual machine runs independently,

31
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.400
even though they share the same physical
server.

32
00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:13.500
This significantly boosts utilization,
often above sixty

33
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.860
percent or more. That means less

34
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.380
hardware doing more work. But that's not
the only

35
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.280
evolution. The next step is

36
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.620
containerization. Unlike virtual machines,

37
00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.940
containers share the host operating system
that

38
00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.660
makes them lighter, faster, and easier to
scale.

39
00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:40.040
Think of containers as highly efficient
compartments, each running a

40
00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.680
single app. Containers are ideal for

41
00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.620
cloud-native applications, microservers,
and workloads that need

42
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.320
rapid scaling. They use fewer resources
than

43
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.180
traditional virtual machines and are
especially useful

44
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.380
in DevOps and modern CI/CD pipelines.

45
00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:03.640
Another way to look at this evolution is
through utilization and redundancy.

46
00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.580
In traditional infrastructure, redundancy
often means duplicating entire

47
00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.600
servers. It's expensive and inefficient.

48
00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.220
Virtualization, and especially
containerization, allows for smarter

49
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.920
redundancy with less hardware. So

50
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.700
how do you choose between physical and
virtual infrastructure?

51
00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:28.240
It mostly comes down to whether you need
dedicated or shared

52
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.800
resources. For highly specialized,

53
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.620
highly performant tasks like scientific
computing or

54
00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.680
real-time finance data, dedicated
hardware, also

55
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.900
called bare metal, gives you maximum power
and control.

56
00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:46.820
But for most other workloads, shared

57
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.620
infrastructure like virtual machines or
containers

58
00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.540
offers greater speed, flexibility, and
better cost savings.

59
00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.220
In summary, physical infrastructure gives
you dedicated hardware

60
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.160
and performance. Virtual machines add
flexibility

61
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.200
by sharing resources. Containers go even
further

62
00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.580
by minimizing overhead and maximizing
portability.

63
00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.380
Understanding this spectrum, physical,
virtual, and

64
00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:19.079
containerized, is key to designing a
modern data

65
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:23.120
center. It's not about one being better
than the other, but about

66
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.340
choosing the right tool for the job.
